// Serving a built directory (site/) as a Cloudflare Worker on its own hostname.
//
//   wrangler deploy
//
// Why this shape:
//   * a Workers custom_domain provisions the DNS record AND the certificate
//     server-side, so it works on a token with Workers scopes + zone:read and
//     no dns_records:edit
//   * NO html_handling override and NO main: let the asset layer serve the URLs
//     natively. Build the site with directory URLs (/Foo/ + Foo/index.html) and
//     then "/" resolves to index.html and /Foo/ to Foo/index.html by itself — no
//     rewrite, no redirect, and nothing on the homepage's critical path that can
//     go stale. (Flat Page.html output forces html_handling "none", which stops
//     "/" mapping to index.html and puts a Worker script in the way; when that
//     script is not running the homepage 404s while /index.html still works.)
//   * `not_found_handling: "404-page"` serves the generated 404.html instead of
//     an empty body. Whatever serves misses, return them with
//     `cache-control: no-store` — a cached 404 is sticky and keeps being served
//     after the path exists.
//
// Keep BOTH route entries: routes take precedence over custom domains, so a
// broader wildcard route owned by another Worker will steal this hostname and
// serve the wrong site with a valid certificate. The longer pattern wins.
//
// Deploying from CI: pin wrangler 4 (cloudflare/wrangler-action@v3 defaults to
// wrangler 3.x, which cannot read this .jsonc file and fails "Missing
// entry-point" as if the config were broken).
{
  "name": "my-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./site",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "404-page"
  },
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "site.example.com", "custom_domain": true },
    { "pattern": "site.example.com/*", "zone_name": "example.com" }
  ]
}
